Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period wilderness drama; naturalistic human faces, real animal fur, and harsh Yukon weather.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Golden California light fills orchards, lawns, and a cement swimming tank. BUCK, a massive four-year-old dog with long brown-and-black fur, a white chest blaze, and a broad wolfish muzzle, carries a child across the grass.

(SOUND: children laughing, water splashing, bees humming)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck lived at Judge Miller's place in the sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here he was king--king over all creeping, crawling, flying things, humans included.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

MANUEL leads Buck from the dark orchard. A STRANGER waits beneath a station lamp, rope in hand.

(SOUND: distant train whistle, coins clinking)

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

The STRANGER loops the rope under Buck's collar. Buck growls; the rope tightens. He leaps, and the stranger throws him hard onto the platform.

(SOUND: Buck choking, locomotive approaching)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Never in all his life had he been so vilely treated, and never in all his life had he been so angry.

INT. SEATTLE BACK YARD - DAY

Buck, bloodshot-eyed and foam-flecked, hurls himself against a splintering crate. A stocky DOG BREAKER in a sagging red sweater calmly raises a club.

(SOUND: hatchet splitting wood, Buck snarling)

DOG BREAKER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck springs through the opening. The club catches him in midair. He crashes, rises, charges again, and falls again.

(SOUND: club striking fur and bone, Buck's ragged roar)

The DOG BREAKER kneels beside the unconscious Buck, then offers water and raw meat.

DOG BREAKER
You've learned your place, and I know mine. Be a good dog and all'll go well.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

EXT. DYEAA BEACH - DAY

Snow falls on a crowded beach. PERREAULT, small and wiry, and FRANCOIS, a broad-shouldered half-breed driver, lead Buck and CURLY from a ship. Buck touches snow with one paw, sniffs it, then licks it and jerks back.

(SOUND: gulls, ship rigging, snow crunching)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was his first snow.

Curly approaches a lean HUSKY. The husky flashes in, tears her face, and leaps away. A silent ring of huskies closes around her when she falls.

(SOUND: snarling pack, Curly's brief scream)

FRANCOIS charges in with an axe, scattering the dogs. Buck watches Curly's still body. SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with sharp black eyes, lolls his tongue.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Once down, that was the end of you.

EXT. DYEAA CAMP - NIGHT

Buck stands harnessed between grim DAVE and one-eyed SOL-LEKS. FRANCOIS snaps the whip; the team drags a sled of firewood through the snow.

(SOUND: harness leather creaking, runners scraping)

FRANCOIS
Mush!

Buck tangles the traces. Dave nips his flank; Sol-leks snaps near his shoulder. Buck quickly steps clear and leans into the breast-band.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

INT. SNOW BURROW - NIGHT

Buck shivers outside the glowing tent while wind sweeps the white camp. He paws at snow, finds BILLEE curled beneath it, then digs himself a hollow beside a spruce bank.

(SOUND: wind rattling canvas, Buck digging snow)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Another lesson. So that was the way they did it.

Buck curls into the burrow. Snow settles over his fur.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

Buck returns to his sheltered snow nest and finds Spitz inside it. Both dogs circle low, teeth bared.

(SOUND: wind knifing across ice, dogs growling)

FRANCOIS
Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

Starving huskies burst from the darkness and raid the food stores. Buck fights three at once, then Spitz bites into Buck's throat from the side.

(SOUND: clubs striking ribs, frantic howling, bacon tins clattering)

PERREAULT and FRANCOIS drive the invaders away. The wounded team staggers into the trees.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
From that moment, Buck's hatred of Spitz was bitter and deathless.

EXT. FROZEN YUKON RIVER - DAY

The team pulls across thin river ice. Buck, leaner now but hard-muscled beneath his long coat, strains in the traces as the ice fractures under the sled.

(SOUND: ice cracking, dogs panting, whip snapping)

SPITZ falls through, dragging the line. Buck braces at the edge while DAVE pulls behind him and FRANCOIS hauls the sled lashings.

(SOUND: rushing black water, men shouting)

Later, FRANCOIS wraps Buck's sore paws in moccasin leather.

FRANCOIS
Wot I say? Dat Buck for sure learn queek as anyt'ing.

EXT. TAHKEENA CREEK - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit darts through moonlit snow. Buck leads a roaring pack in pursuit, his fur streaming, his eyes fixed on living prey.

(SOUND: pounding paws, rabbit squealing, wolfish cries)

Spitz cuts across the creek bend and breaks the rabbit's back. Buck slams shoulder-first into him.

(SOUND: teeth clacking, dogs forming a silent circle)

They circle in moonlight. Buck feints for Spitz's throat, dives low, and breaks Spitz's foreleg with a crunch.

(SOUND: bone cracking, Spitz's howl)

Buck breaks the other foreleg. The pack surges forward as Buck turns away, standing victorious in the snow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

At harness-up, Buck takes the lead position. FRANCOIS tries to place Sol-leks there, but Buck drives the older dog aside and keeps beyond reach of the club.

(SOUND: jangling harness, dogs barking, wind over snow)

PERREAULT
T'row down de club.

FRANCOIS drops it. Buck trots directly into the lead traces.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck!

The team launches forward as one body, Buck leading with rigid confidence.

EXT. CASSIAR BAR TRAIL - DAWN

A mail team crawls through blue dawn. DAVE, gaunt and shaking, falls in the traces. The SCOTCH DRIVER removes his harness, but Dave bites through Sol-leks's traces and takes his old place.

(SOUND: bells tinkling, Dave whimpering, sled runners grinding)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The pride of trace and trail was his.

At camp, Dave lies gasping in the snow as the team departs. A revolver shot rings out behind the river timber.

(SOUND: single gunshot echoing through trees)

Buck lowers his head but continues pulling.

EXT. WHITE RIVER BANK - DAY

Spring water runs beneath rotten ice. Buck is skeletal now: limp fur, exposed ribs, white chest blaze dulled with mud and blood. HAL beats him with a club while MERCEDES cries nearby and CHARLES watches helplessly.

(SOUND: club blows, melting ice trickling, Mercedes sobbing)

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

Buck remains down. JOHN THORNTON, a weathered, limping prospector in a fur cap and buckskin coat, rushes in and strikes Hal aside with an axe handle.

(SOUND: Hal hitting the snow, Thornton's furious breath)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

Thornton cuts Buck free. Hal, Charles, and Mercedes drive the remaining team onto the ice.

The river opens beneath them. Sled, dogs, and humans vanish into black water.

(SOUND: ice shearing, Mercedes's fading scream, river rushing)

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks Thornton's hand.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S RIVER CAMP - DAY

Buck, restored to powerful condition, lies beside Thornton's fire. His thick brown-and-black coat shines again; the white blaze runs down his chest. SKEET cleans a scar on his shoulder while NIG dozes nearby.

(SOUND: river murmuring, fire popping, birds calling)

JOHN THORNTON cups Buck's massive head in both hands and rests his forehead against it.

JOHN THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck gently closes his jaws around Thornton's mittened hand, then releases it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Thornton's poling boat flips in violent rapids. JOHN clings to a slick rock while HANS and PETE secure a rope on shore. Buck races along the bank.

(SOUND: rapids roaring, rope snapping taut, men shouting)

JOHN THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Buck swims back to shore, then lets Hans fasten the rope around his chest and shoulders.

(SOUND: Buck splashing into current, Hans paying out rope)

Buck drives straight into the torrent. Thornton catches his shaggy neck. Hans snubs the line, and man and dog are dragged beneath the foam.

(SOUND: water hammering rocks, rope groaning)

On shore, Thornton wakes beside Buck's limp body and checks his ribs.

JOHN THORNTON
We camp right here.

EXT. DAWSON MAIN STREET - DAY

A thousand-pound sled of flour stands frozen to packed snow. Fur-clad miners crowd around. Buck, broad-chested and magnificent, is hitched alone to the traces while Thornton kneels beside him.

(SOUND: crowd murmuring, harness jingling, distant saloon piano)

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck grips Thornton's mitten gently in his teeth. Thornton steps back.

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck jerks right. The runners crack loose.

(SOUND: ice snapping under runners, crowd drawing breath)

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck jerks left. The sled pivots free.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, mush!

Buck throws every muscle into the traces. The heavy sled lurches, then moves steadily past the hundred-yard marker.

(SOUND: runners grinding, crowd erupting in cheers)

Thornton falls to his knees and embraces Buck.

JOHN THORNTON
You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

Moose-hide bags of gold dust sit beside a spruce-bough lodge. Buck lies by the fire, older, huge, and fully furred, while Thornton, Hans, and Pete sleep nearby.

(SOUND: fire crackling, distant wolf howl, wind in spruce)

A long, lean TIMBER WOLF howls from the forest. Buck rises and slips between the trees.

EXT. FOREST CREEK BED - NIGHT

Buck follows the timber wolf through moonlit woods. They circle, sniff noses, then run side by side over the divide.

(SOUND: paws over moss, flowing creek, two dogs breathing)

At a stream, Buck stops. He looks back toward the distant campfire glow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck remembered John Thornton.

The wolf gives a mournful howl. Buck turns and runs home.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - DAY

Buck returns from the forest with his coat thick and wild, brown muzzle markings sharp above his white chest blaze. He follows a trail into camp. NIG lies dead in brush, pierced by an arrow.

(SOUND: flies buzzing, distant chanting, Buck's low growl)

Buck finds HANS dead beside the ruined lodge. YEEHAT HUNTERS dance around the wreckage.

(SOUND: Yeehat chant, arrows rattling, fire crackling)

Buck explodes into the clearing. He tears down the nearest hunter, then races through the others in a blur of fur and teeth.

(SOUND: panicked shouting, arrows striking trees, Buck roaring)

The hunters flee into the forest. Buck returns to the muddy pool and finds SKEET at its edge. He follows Thornton's scent into the water; no trail comes out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - NIGHT

Moonlight fills the ruined clearing. A wolf pack pours from the forest. Buck stands motionless beside the pool, huge and scarred, his long coat bright under the moon.

(SOUND: wolves yelping, night wind moving through spruce)

The boldest wolf leaps. Buck strikes and breaks its neck. The pack circles, then falls back.

(SOUND: snarls fading into cautious whines, water lapping the bank)

The timber wolf approaches. Buck recognizes him. They touch noses. An old battle-scarred wolf lifts his muzzle and howls.

(SOUND: one long wolf howl answered by the pack)

Buck sits, raises his head, and joins the howl.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And Buck ran with them, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he ran.

The pack disappears into the moonlit forest, Buck at its head.

(SOUND: running paws fading beneath the wolf chorus)

FADE OUT.

THE END
